Context: this is part of Grothendieck's 'variations sur l'équation des lacets', the 1980s sequel to La Longue Marche. It studies a profinite group 𝔊 (in the universal case essentially π1 of P^1 minus {0,1,∞}, free on ρ,σ). The structure is a pair (𝔊, 𝔊^φ) together with:
- an extension 𝔊^+;
- a subgroup L_0;
- the group Z_{ρ,σ} of data u = (u, μ, ε2, ε3), where u ∈ Aut(𝔊^+) normalises L_0 and sends ρ, σ to 𝔊^φ-conjugates of powers of themselves, subject to the 'loop relation';
- the groups G_{ρ,σ} and G^𝔊_{ρ,σ}, obtained from Z_{ρ,σ} acting on 𝔊^+ and the map L_0 ⊂ Z_{ρ,σ} → 𝔊^+ by a 'standard construction'. This is presumably the semidirect product 𝔊^+ ⋊ Z modulo the antidiagonal copy of L_0, as with outer and automorphism group extensions.

Section 6°, 'Fonctorialités', starts on the previous page. It takes a surjection φ: 𝔊 → 𝔊' (91) compatible with the distinguished subgroups. The admissibility condition is that every u ∈ Z_{ρ,σ} stabilises Ker φ, so that u descends to a unique u' (diagram 92).

This page draws the consequences:
- (93) a homomorphism φ_Z: Z_{ρ,σ} → Z_{ρ',σ'}, compatible with the actions on 𝔊^+ and 𝔊'^+ and with the maps L_0 → 𝔊^+ and L_0' → 𝔊'^+;
- by functoriality of the standard construction, homomorphisms (94) G_{ρ,σ} → G_{ρ',σ'} and (95) G^𝔊_{ρ,σ} → G^𝔊_{ρ',σ'};
- hence a morphism of the diagrams (64) attached to (ρ,σ) into those attached to (ρ',σ').

Grothendieck concludes that all diagram constructions of the earlier sections are functorial for admissible morphisms.

Remark (96)–(98): suppose 𝔊' has the following lifting property. Whenever α', β' ∈ 𝔊'^𝔊_ε satisfy the loop equation [α',ρ] = [β',σ] ε1^{μ-1} for a suitable μ ∈ Ẑ, there exists u' ∈ Aut(𝔊'^+) with u'(ρ) = α'(ρ) and u'(σ) = β'(σ). Equivalently, α'^{-1}u' centralises ρ and β'^{-1}u' centralises σ.

The next page completes this: the loop relation translates into u' normalising L_0'. Then every (ρ,σ)-morphism into 𝔊' is automatically admissible. This is a 'freeness/rigidity' hypothesis, of the kind that holds for the free profinite group on ρ,σ. It is the same mechanism by which a GT-type pair (λ, f) defines an endomorphism ρ ↦ ρ^λ, σ ↦ f^{-1}σ^λ f of F̂_2. Grothendieck announces he will explain this in the universal case.
